syscall: force ENOSYS for openat2 so callers fall back to openat

GNU tar 1.34 (and other recent coreutils/gnulib users) open directories
with the raw openat2() syscall using RESOLVE_BENEATH, called via
syscall(SYS_openat2, ...). pseudo has no openat2 wrapper and its syscall()
interceptor passes unknown numbers straight through, so the directory fd
returned by openat2() is never recorded in pseudo's client-side fd->path
table. A subsequent openat(dirfd, name, O_CREAT) then fails path
resolution (fd_path() returns NULL -> "unknown directory" ->
"couldn't allocate absolute path"), and the real openat() is issued with a
bad path, returning EFAULT ("Bad address").

Under OE this breaks perform_packagecopy()'s
  tar --exclude=./sysroot-only -cf - -C ${D} -p -S . | tar -xf - -C ${PKGD}
which runs under pseudo, so do_package fails and no packages/debs can be
produced on hosts whose tar uses openat2().

Report ENOSYS for SYS_openat2, exactly as pseudo already does for
SYS_renameat2. gnulib treats openat2()==ENOSYS as "kernel too old" and
transparently falls back to a plain openat() emulation, which pseudo does
wrap and track correctly.

Upstream-Status: Pending

Signed-off-by: yanan.he <yanan.he@example.com>
---
 ports/linux/pseudo_wrappers.c | 14 ++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

--- a/ports/linux/pseudo_wrappers.c
+++ b/ports/linux/pseudo_wrappers.c
@@ -78,6 +78,20 @@
 	(void) number;
 #endif

+#ifdef SYS_openat2
+	/* GNU tar 1.34+ (and other coreutils) open directories via a raw
+	 * syscall(SYS_openat2, ..., RESOLVE_BENEATH). pseudo cannot record the
+	 * returned dirfd (no openat2 wrapper), so a later openat(dirfd, name)
+	 * fails path resolution and returns EFAULT. Report ENOSYS so callers
+	 * (gnulib) fall back to the wrapped openat(), which pseudo tracks. This
+	 * mirrors the SYS_renameat2 handling above.
+	 */
+	if (number == SYS_openat2) {
+		errno = ENOSYS;
+		return -1;
+	}
+#endif
+
 #ifdef SYS_seccomp
 	/* pseudo and seccomp are incompatible as pseudo uses different syscalls
 	 * so pretend to enable seccomp but really do nothing */
